"""Generate interpretation from fixed Stage 4.5 outputs."""
import sys,json,time,hashlib
from pathlib import Path
import numpy as np,pandas as pd
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];sys.path[:0]=[str(ROOT/'scripts'),str(ROOT/'notebooks'),str(ROOT)]
from anecdote_common_1029 import *
P=ROOT/'reports/stage4_5/readout_knn_discrepancy_1029'
def main():
 s=pd.read_csv(P/'integrated_summary.csv').set_index('method'); km=pd.read_csv(P/'knn_metrics.csv');nm=pd.read_csv(P/'native_hidden_knn_metrics.csv');null=pd.read_csv(P/'permutation_null_metrics.csv');paired=pd.read_csv(P/'paired_comparisons.csv');ids=json.loads((P/'asset_and_identity_audit.json').read_text());replay=json.loads((P/'native_extraction.json').read_text());repro=json.loads((P/'knn_reproduction_checks.json').read_text());dist=json.loads((P/'query_redshift_distribution.json').read_text());neighbor=json.loads((P/'original_neighbourhood_summary.json').read_text());nh=json.loads((P/'native_head_training.json').read_text());
 def pct(n):return f'{100*float(n):.2f}%'
 def val(name,key):return float(s.loc[name,key])
 def rate(name):return pct(val(name,'outlier_mean'))
 def ratek(name):return pct(km[km.method==name].outlier_fraction.mean())
 def pair(a,b,metric='outlier'):
  x=paired[(paired.left==a)&(paired.right==b)&(paired.metric==metric)].iloc[0];return x
 def interval(a,b):
  x=pair(a,b);return f"{100*x.delta_mean:+.2f} pp (paired galaxy 95% CI {100*x.bootstrap_low:+.2f} to {100*x.bootstrap_high:+.2f} pp)"
 def row(name,label):
  x=s.loc[name];return f"| {label} | {int(x.train_labels)} | {int(x.dev_labels)} | {100*x.outlier_mean:.2f}% | {x.nmad_mean:.4f} | {x.ce_mean:.3f} | {x.crps_mean:.4f} | {x.coverage_mean:.3f} |"
 source=[row('BS_historical_native','BS historical / native'),row('BS_historical_canonical','BS historical / canonical'),row('BS_hard','BS fresh / canonical / hard'),row('BS_smooth3','BS fresh / canonical / sigma3'),row('BS_native_smooth3','BS fresh / native / sigma3'),row('B0_smooth3','B0 fresh / canonical / sigma3'),row('BC_smooth3','BC fresh / canonical / sigma3'),row('NUM_smooth3','Numerical MLP / sigma3'),row('photo_original','Engineered photometry kNN'),row('hidden_BS_euclidean','BS last-token hidden kNN'),row('hidden_B0_euclidean','B0 last-token hidden kNN'),row('hidden_BC_euclidean','BC last-token hidden kNN'),row('hidden_BS_meanpool_euclidean','BS mean-pooling hidden kNN'),row('BS_native_hidden_euclidean','BS native-input hidden kNN')]
 for name in ['constant_median','constant_mean']:
  g=km[km.method==name];source.append(f"| {name} | 256 | 0 | {100*g.outlier_fraction.mean():.2f}% | {g.nmad.mean():.4f} | — | — | — |")
 table='\n'.join(source);minsep=ids['identity']['support_pool__query']['min_arcsec'];bounds=(min(r['best_update'] for r in nh['results']),max(r['best_update'] for r in nh['results']))
 text=f'''# Paper II Stage 4.5 — native head, sparse readout and kNN diagnostic

## Status and scope

Completed retrospectively on the exact Stage 4 GMS query cohort (1,024 galaxies). Stage 4 query results were already inspected before this follow-up was designed: it is exploratory, not a new independent test. The same three nested K=256 subsets and six subset/head-seed combinations were used throughout. K=256 means **256 gradient-training labels plus 256 fixed development labels** for learned heads; kNN and constants use 256 support labels and no development labels. Historical BS previously trained on 22,444 spectroscopic galaxies; “0 additional labels” does not mean it had no prior supervision.

## Matched-cohort results

| Configuration | Additional training labels | Development labels | Outliers | NMAD | CE | CRPS | 68% coverage |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
{table}

The table shows means over six fits for learned heads and three subsets for kNN. Native historical models are a single fixed prediction. Fit ranges and per-fit records are in [integrated_summary.csv](integrated_summary.csv) and [integrated_model_metrics.csv](integrated_model_metrics.csv). Historical and Stage 4 BS grids are both 300 bins from 0 to 4.5; CE here uses bin masses on that common grid. The constant point predictors have no PDF.

![Same-cohort results](figure1_same_cohort.png)

## Ten requested answers

1. **Original native BS:** the matched historical test predictions give {rate('BS_historical_native')} outliers, NMAD {val('BS_historical_native','nmad_mean'):.4f}, CE {val('BS_historical_native','ce_mean'):.3f}, CRPS {val('BS_historical_native','crps_mean'):.4f}, and {100*val('BS_historical_native','coverage_mean'):.1f}% nominal 68% coverage. All 1,024 IDs and labels align with the Stage 4 query. Thirty-two query examples were freshly replayed bit-exactly, in addition to the historical validation replay. The full 1,024 prediction set is archived inference, not a claim that all 1,024 were newly rerun.
2. **Serialization:** the same historical head on canonical cached hidden states has {rate('BS_historical_canonical')} outliers. Native minus canonical is {interval('BS_historical_native','BS_historical_canonical')}. This is a large effect of the *whole input interface*, including token order, precision and final marker; it does not isolate an individual string component. Historical head architecture/training cannot be equated with a fresh small head.
3. **Sparse readout:** BS hard→sigma3 changes {rate('BS_hard')} to {rate('BS_smooth3')}; paired sigma3 minus hard is {interval('BS_smooth3','BS_hard')}. B0 and the numerical MLP improve too; soft targets fix a general sparse-classification problem. Native features with a fresh sigma3 head give {rate('BS_native_smooth3')}, versus {rate('BS_smooth3')} on canonical features; paired native minus canonical is {interval('BS_native_smooth3','BS_smooth3')}. Against the photometric kNN, the native fresh head has difference {interval('BS_native_smooth3','photo_original')}. That 2×2 cross does not identify a unique causal percentage because historical head capacity and prior training differ. The six native heads selected updates {bounds[0]}–{bounds[1]}.
4. **BS hidden kNN versus fresh BS:** fixed uniform-k10 last-token Euclidean BS hidden kNN gives {rate('hidden_BS_euclidean')}, versus {rate('BS_smooth3')} for the fresh soft head. A negative conclusion is confined to this fixed neighbour metric. The already cached mean-pooled BS feature produces {rate('hidden_BS_meanpool_euclidean')}, illustrating that token aggregation affects local geometry. Native-input BS hidden kNN is {rate('BS_native_hidden_euclidean')} under the same uniform Euclidean protocol, compared with {rate('hidden_BS_euclidean')} for canonical input. Passing native features through the frozen, historically supervised BS head's penultimate layer gives {rate('BS_native_supervised_head_penultimate')}; its canonical counterpart gives {rate('BS_supervised_head_penultimate_canonical')}. These head-derived vectors inherit prior spec-z supervision and are not pure backbone features.
5. **BS versus B0 neighbourhoods:** last-token BS versus B0 Euclidean is {rate('hidden_BS_euclidean')} versus {rate('hidden_B0_euclidean')}; BS minus B0 is {interval('hidden_BS_euclidean','hidden_B0_euclidean')}. Centered-cosine results are {rate('hidden_BS_cosine')} versus {rate('hidden_B0_cosine')}. Mean-pool differences are reported separately. The same query and matching support subsets are used; this is an in-domain, conditional geometry comparison.
6. **Why photometric kNN is strong:** the exact engineered feature kNN reproduces {rate('photo_original')}; constant median gives {ratek('constant_median')}, and permuting support-feature/redshift pairs gives mean {100*null.outlier_fraction.mean():.2f}% (60 permutations over three subsets). Query redshifts are concentrated at median {dist['median']:.3f} and 5th–95th percentiles {dist['q05']:.3f}–{dist['q95']:.3f}, but the constants and shuffle show that object-specific photometric association contributes. Shape/mask alone gives {ratek('shape_mask')}, apparent brightness alone {ratek('brightness_only')}, together {ratek('shape_brightness')}, and adding relative errors {ratek('shape_brightness_relative_error')}. Measurement/selection-only features give {ratek('measurement_selection_only')}. These are predictive ablations, not causal fractions.
7. **Integrity:** all three original kNN PDFs reproduce the saved values within {max(r['pdf_max_difference'] for r in repro):.2g}; changing query labels and permuting query order leaves predictions invariant. No shared object/group identity, no support–query pair within 2 arcsec (minimum {minsep:.2f} arcsec), and no known BS/BC historical train/validation match or ambiguous exposure were found. Traceable input fields are measured flux/error, masks, quality, EBV and brightness; no target-redshift proxy enters the implemented features. This is “no identified leakage,” not proof of complete absence or foundation-pretraining isolation.
8. **What explains the old comparison:** large native/canonical and hard/sigma3 effects, plus shape/brightness signal and differences among distance/pooling choices, all have direct support. Their interactions and historical supervision prevent attributing an additive fraction of the BS–kNN discrepancy to each. Existing soft MLP is {rate('NUM_smooth3')} versus kNN {rate('photo_original')}; MLP minus kNN is {interval('NUM_smooth3','photo_original')}. The paired interval, not the ranking of means, should guide interpretation.
9. **BC→GMS:** the original hard-head BC−B0 paired comparison remains interpretable under that predeclared readout, provided known audit limits are stated. Under sigma3, BC−B0 is {interval('BC_smooth3','B0_smooth3')}; original Stage 4 reports six of six paired CE intervals below zero, but only one of six outlier intervals. It is a readout/metric-dependent conditional transfer signal, not superiority to simple numerical models. No new independent external generalisation is established.
10. **Minimum next CLAUDS experiment:** lock one serialization/readout and strong numeric baselines *before* accessing an independent, previously unexamined CLAUDS spectroscopic cohort; audit prior identity exposure, measure matched K + development supervision, and evaluate the same objects with paired intervals. Explicitly characterize magnitude/redshift selection. A bright spectroscopic subset does not validate the whole faint CLAUDS population.

## Neighbourhoods, selection and robustness

The original kNN uses support-only coordinate standardization, k=10, inverse-distance weights and a fixed Gaussian redshift KDE. It is **engineered photometric-feature kNN**, not a bare colour or raw-flux distance. The uniform-weight comparison applies the same K-only transforms and KDE to photometry and all hidden feature spaces. Last-token cosine uses K-centered unit vectors; Euclidean uses K-standardized coordinates without L2 normalization. No k, loss width or distance was picked using the new query results. Mean pooling and historical-head latent vectors are named sensitivity controls; the latter contains prior supervised training and is not a pure backbone representation.

Original weighted kNN nearest-neighbour distance median {neighbor['nearest_distance']['0.5']:.4f}; nearest angular separation median {neighbor['nearest_angular_arcsec']['0.5']:.1f} arcsec; median |Δz| {neighbor['nearest_abs_dz']['0.5']:.4f}; |Δz|<0.05 fraction {100*neighbor['nearest_abs_dz_lt_0.05']:.1f}%; median effective neighbour count {neighbor['effective_neighbours']['0.5']:.2f}. Details, including all neighbour IDs, distances and labels for each query, are in [neighbourhood_diagnostics.csv](neighbourhood_diagnostics.csv). Conditional outliers versus distance and apparent i magnitude are in [original_knn_conditioned_metrics.csv](original_knn_conditioned_metrics.csv); explicit nearest photometric/positional differences are in [nearest_neighbour_photometric_differences.csv](nearest_neighbour_photometric_differences.csv).

![Neighbourhoods](figure2_neighbourhoods.png)

Redshift-stratified query counts are {dist['per_bin_query_counts']} across the figure bins; the upper tail is small. A single catastrophic-outlier threshold can hide different error tails. The raw conditionals and counts are in [conditional_redshift_metrics.csv](conditional_redshift_metrics.csv), original BS training and support/query counts are in [redshift_source_support_counts.csv](redshift_source_support_counts.csv), the distributions are in [redshift_distribution.png](redshift_distribution.png), and the pre-fixed threshold curves in [error_threshold_tail.csv](error_threshold_tail.csv).

![Failures and error tails](figure3_redshift_and_tails.png)

![Head learning and coverage](figure4_learning_curves.png)

The histories plot hard CE for both training conditions, matching the saved Stage 4 checkpoints. The sigma3 training target was centered on the **integer true-redshift bin**, and checkpoint selection used development hard CE. No unrecorded soft-training-loss curves are invented.

## Hypothesis assessment

| Hypothesis | Assessment | Evidence and limitation |
|---|---|---|
| Historical native head retains useful prediction | Supported | {rate('BS_historical_native')} matched-cohort outliers; trained with substantial prior labels. |
| Native-to-canonical interface shift | Supported for this entire interface change | Same old head: {rate('BS_historical_native')}→{rate('BS_historical_canonical')}; individual token/field causes remain unresolved. |
| Sparse hard-classifier readout | Supported | BS hard→sigma3 {rate('BS_hard')}→{rate('BS_smooth3')}; improvement also appears in B0 and NUM. |
| Hidden-neighbourhood geometry | Partially supported and metric-dependent | Last-token BS Euclidean {rate('hidden_BS_euclidean')}; mean-pool {rate('hidden_BS_meanpool_euclidean')}; these do not measure all recoverable information. |
| Query redshift concentration alone | Not sufficient | Constants {ratek('constant_median')}; shuffled mean {100*null.outlier_fraction.mean():.2f}% versus original {rate('photo_original')}. |
| Engineered photometric features help | Supported as predictive association | Shape/brightness/error ablations and shuffle; population selection remains a limitation. |
| Identity/feature leakage | No leakage identified within audited sources | Exact/positional/label perturbation audits pass; unknown foundation pretraining and undetected catalogue issues remain. |

## Statistical accounting and provenance

[paired_comparisons.csv](paired_comparisons.csv) uses 1,000 galaxy bootstrap draws with the same sampled query indices across all fixed fits, averaging matched subset/initialization differences within each draw. Repeated predictions of the same galaxy are not treated as independent samples. Fit minima/maxima are descriptive training/subset ranges, not galaxy confidence intervals. For outlier comparisons the table also records both-wrong and discordant counts. A few hard/fresh heads are correlated because support subsets overlap; no inference treats them as six independent cohorts.

Check A uses a full archived test prediction matched by persistent IDs, a newly evaluated exact 32-query replay, and frozen native features for all 2,304 clean objects. Check B replays all 48 existing GMS hard/sigma3 PDFs with maximum absolute discrepancy {ids['maximum_head_pdf_replay_error']:.2g}. Check C uses saved no-grad canonical/mean representations and the new native cache. Check D reproduces the fixed original kNN, audits identities and exposures, runs constants, feature ablations and pre-fixed label permutations. See [checkpoint replay](checkpoint_replay_and_native_head.md), [kNN integrity audit](knn_leakage_and_features_audit.md), [protocol.json](protocol.json), and [reproduction commands](commands_executed.sh). No backbone, adapter or historical head was trained or modified; six new native-input heads are the only new gradient fits. No Stage 2C was launched.
'''
 (P/'scientific_assessment.md').write_text(text)
 (P/'checkpoint_replay_and_native_head.md').write_text(f'''# BS checkpoint and native replay\n\nBS base, adapter and original head were loaded frozen using the Stage 4 provenance. Native strings were reconstructed from source HDF feature rows matched by ID and compared to Stage 4 photometry; {replay['native_token_audit']['n']} clean objects fit within the historical 512-token limit (maximum {replay['native_token_audit']['max']}, zero truncated).\n\nThe historical 32-validation-example replay and the additional 32-query-example replay both pass with zero maximum logit and PDF discrepancy. Every clean object belongs to historical test, and all 1,024 matched query redshifts are identical to archived truth. Native query predictions are the archived outputs with an audited exact subset replay; the native cache itself was newly computed.\n\nNative BS outliers {rate('BS_historical_native')}; canonical BS historical head {rate('BS_historical_canonical')}. All output uses the 0–4.5, 300-bin grid. Full metrics: [native_vs_canonical_metrics.csv](native_vs_canonical_metrics.csv). Detailed manifest: [native_extraction.json](native_extraction.json).\n''')
 (P/'knn_leakage_and_features_audit.md').write_text(f'''# Engineered photometric kNN integrity audit\n\nGMS raw source is HSC grizy observed flux/error and MW EBV, joined on persistent target ID; the Stage 4 numeric path produces scaled flux/error/SNR, mask, quality/availability, brightness and EBV/availability. It contains no input target redshift, ZPHOT, supervised redshift estimate or template-fit output. Support-only mean/STD with floor and clipping, k=10, inverse-distance weights and the fixed redshift KDE exactly reproduce the saved Stage 4 PDFs, maximum discrepancy {max(r['pdf_max_difference'] for r in repro):.2g}. The query-label and query-row perturbation tests pass. Source fields and selection metadata may carry observational/population information; absence of identified leakage is not a proof of its impossibility.\n\nThere are no cross-role shared IDs/groups, no support/query match within 2 arcsec (minimum {minsep:.2f}), and no identified BS/BC historical train or validation exposure/ambiguity. See [asset_and_identity_audit.json](asset_and_identity_audit.json) and [knn_reproduction_checks.json](knn_reproduction_checks.json).\n\nOriginal {rate('photo_original')}; constant median {ratek('constant_median')}; 60 fixed support-label permutations average {100*null.outlier_fraction.mean():.2f}%. Normalized shape {ratek('shape_mask')}; brightness {ratek('brightness_only')}; their union {ratek('shape_brightness')}; quality/selection-only {ratek('measurement_selection_only')}. See [knn_metrics.csv](knn_metrics.csv), [permutation_null_metrics.csv](permutation_null_metrics.csv) and the [full assessment](scientific_assessment.md).\n''')
 (P/'summary_zh.md').write_text(f'''# GMS kNN 差距诊断：摘要\n\n相同 1,024 个查询对象上，BS 历史原生接口离群率 **{rate('BS_historical_native')}**，历史头接 Stage 4 canonical 输入为 **{rate('BS_historical_canonical')}**。32 个查询对象的新推理与历史归档逐值一致；全量结果按 ID 复用历史预测。\n\nBS 新头从硬标签 **{rate('BS_hard')}** 改为 sigma3 后为 **{rate('BS_smooth3')}**；原生输入上的新 sigma3 头为 **{rate('BS_native_smooth3')}**。数值 MLP sigma3 为 **{rate('NUM_smooth3')}**，原光度 kNN 为 **{rate('photo_original')}**；MLP 与 kNN 的配对差异为 {interval('NUM_smooth3','photo_original')}。训练型头另用 256 个开发标签，kNN 不使用开发标签。\n\n最后 token 欧氏 kNN：BS **{rate('hidden_BS_euclidean')}**，B0 **{rate('hidden_B0_euclidean')}**；BS 平均池化 kNN **{rate('hidden_BS_meanpool_euclidean')}**；BS 原生输入的隐藏空间 kNN 为 **{rate('BS_native_hidden_euclidean')}**。几何表现依赖所选读出，不能直接等同于骨干信息总量。\n\n原 kNN 精确复现；无共享身份或已识别的历史监督曝光。常数中位数离群率 {ratek('constant_median')}，打乱特征—红移配对后平均 {100*null.outlier_fraction.mean():.2f}%，支持光度与对象红移关联确实有用。未发现泄漏，不等于证明不可能有未识别的泄漏。\n\n该查询集此前已检查，整个研究为回顾性诊断。BS 历史头拥有 22,444 个历史光谱训练标签；接口、头容量和既有监督共同变化，不能把误差差距拆成精确因果比例。\n\n[完整报告](scientific_assessment.md) · [同队列主图](figure1_same_cohort.png) · [配对比较](paired_comparisons.csv)\n''')
 print('WRITEUP COMPLETE',flush=True)
if __name__=='__main__':main()
